          "source_quote": "ἵν᾿ ὃ τοῖς προτέροις χρόνοις οὐκ εἰργάσαντο, νῦν ἀναδράμωσιν τὰς ἡμέρας καὶ ἀγαθόν τι ποιήσωσιν.",
          "english_quote": "They must now make up the days and do some good that they did not do before.",
          "issue": "The source presents making up the days and doing good as the purpose of the swift repentance, using ἵνα with subjunctive verbs. The simplified edition replaces that purpose relationship with a separate explicit obligation, “They must.” This adds modal force to this clause and leaves its purpose connection unexpressed; the standard edition preserves that connection.",
          "suggested_revision": "A candidate for fresh review is: “This is so they can now make up the days and do some good that they did not do before.” Preserve the connection to swift repentance rather than introducing a separate “must” statement.",
